// PR #392: `codegen_short_circuit` fed its right operand straight into
// an i8 PHI without the bool check its left operand already goes
// through, so a non-bool right operand silently emitted invalid LLVM IR
// instead of failing to compile.
//
// The operand used to be a `std::shared_ptr<int>`, on the grounds that
// "scpp has no implicit conversion to `bool`". That is the [conv.bool]
// *standard* conversion, which spec §16.3(4) does remove -- but
// [expr.log.and]/1 contextually converts each operand to `bool`, and
// for an operand of class type that is a call to a conversion function
// ([class.conv.fct], [over.match.conv]), which no clause of the spec
// modifies. `std::shared_ptr` declares `explicit operator bool`
// ([util.smartptr.shared.obs]/8), so `flag && shared` is well-formed --
// clang++-22 -std=c++26 accepts it and so does scpp now. `Opaque`
// declares no conversion function at all, which is what makes this
// operand genuinely unusable as a condition and keeps the check PR #392
// added under test.
import std;

struct Opaque {
    int v;
};

int main() {
    bool flag = true;
    Opaque opaque{4};
    bool result = flag && opaque;
    return result ? 0 : 1;
}
